===Etymology 2===
From the noun {{m|en|shine}}, or perhaps continuing {{der|en|enm|schinen}} in its causative uses, from {{der|en|ang|scīn|t=brightness, shine}}, and also {{inh|en|enm|schenen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|scǣnan|t=to render brilliant, make shine}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skainijaną}}, causative of {{m|gem-pro|*skīnaną|t=to shine}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (something) to be smooth and shiny by rubbing; put a shine on (something); [[polish]] (something).
#: {{syn|en|wax|buff|polish|furbish|burnish|smooth|smoothen}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''shined''' my shoes until they were polished smooth and gleaming.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|cricket}} To [[polish]] a [[cricket ball]] using [[saliva]] and one’s [[clothing]].

